  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1054 PM CDT MON AUG 31 2026\n\nSimilar to the last few nights, water vapor imagery reveals a\nstout upper level ridge expanding across the southeastern US.\nNewly formed Tropical Storm Edouard continues to move slowly to\nthe north-northwest over the Northern Gulf. This system is\nexpected to make landfall along the TX/LA border late Tuesday\nafternoon or early evening. Increasing subsidence will help\nmaintain dry conditions across the area through the majority of\nthe work week. We look to continue with low chances for diurnally\ndriven convection across our southern areas where moisture\nremains elevated closer to the coast. At this time, we are not\nexpecting much in the way of severe weather as wind shear will be\nminimal. However, instability will be sufficient enough to\nsupport a stronger storm or two as we reach peak heating. Low to\nmedium (20-50%) chances for showers and storms return to the\nforecast for the southern half of the state late Thursday through\nthe weekend as a shortwave rotates west along the southern\nperiphery of the ridge.\n\nA warming trend kicks in over the coming days as temperatures\nclimb back into the mid to upper 90s by mid week. Humid\nconditions will push heat indices into the 105-108 range each \nafternoon. Daily Heat Advisories will likely be needed by \nWednesday. Overall, this will promote a significant heat risk for \nthe region. The latest probabilistic heat risk data suggests a 60-\n80% chance of a major or higher heat risk as we head into Labor \nDay Weekend. We will increase heat safety messaging throughout the\nweek; emphasize the importance of hydration and limiting outdoor \nexposure.\n\n95/Castillo",
